    "turn_text": "Yeah. And I got to say, obviously, that's the actual reality here. We are covering it. We'll be. tracking the entirety of both conventions, but I'm not going to be leaving Washington DC. I had dreams of a week in Milwaukee, a week in Charlotte, or Jacksonville, or wherever the hell it was going to be in the Republican side. And all virtual, we've gotten Rick, you and I kind of a detailed run through from the Democrats about what their convention is going to look like this week. We've spoken a lot with the Trump campaign about what their convention is going to look like. What I find fascinating here and we'll see how it plays out over the next two weeks back to back conventions is the democrats have basically been resigned to the fact that their convention or at least most of it would be virtual for months for at least three months maybe even longer And they've been planning for it. They've been shooting videos. About half of this convention, this virtual convention will be on tape. They've, you know, tapped, you know, they're going to have live remotes across much of the country over the course of the programming they're going to be putting together. And Republicans on the other side, the Trump campaign had been clinging to the belief that they were going to have a real convention, an in-person convention, until just really just a couple of weeks ago. So there's a bit of a scramble on the Republican side to put something together. So I think that one of the interesting things here is You know, are the Democrats going to get a real big bang for their buck out of the fact that they've been, you know, so meticulously planning for this thing with a, you know, rather elaborate programming for each of the four nights? Are they going to get a, you know, a boost out of that, you know, and our Republicans are going to be",
