    "turn_idx": 12,
    "turn_text": "Democrats, and we remember the plagiarism issue with Michelle Obama and the very long Trump speech, but all of that was was made for just really compelling television, that said, it is television. And to me, there's a certain intellectual honesty in recognizing that these are just television programs at this stage, that neither candidate was going to face any kind of a challenge to to the nomination, the idea of a contested convention faded many, many months ago, even on the Democratic side. Yeah, there's always talk about platforms and the direction of the party and those sort of things, but that's secondary. These are, and they've become, over the last few decades, increasingly anachronistic, and they are now television shows, and they're messaging opportunities, and now you strip it down to its very basics, and that's all we have. Two hours a night for four nights for the Democrats this week, four nights for the Republicans next week. What do you cram into that? Who do you showcase? What are the sound bites? What are the bells and whistles? What does it look like? And how do you get that message across? I don't know who's watching. I don't know what the upshot of these speeches will be. I do know that we'll be there covering them and watching them and analyzing them online and on television. And, you know, it's a turn to politics. If nothing else, at least, it gets the country in the mood again for an election, because there's been so much else in the way of everything. I feel like conventions will kick it off. Then we've got Labor Day and debates. And before you know it, we're in November.",
