  {
    "episode_id": 77270,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/7/77270.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 13,
    "turn_text": "Now, the Democrats have told us that they've got, I mean, it's this lineup of all the big power players in the party, Michelle Obama. Bill Clinton of Barack Obama, obviously Kamala Harris and Biden and the rising stars of the party AOC. This is a packed lineup, but the question that I asked was, will any of these speeches, any of them, have an actual audience. Obviously we're in the COVID-19 era, social distancing is a requirement, but I could conceive a scenario where we've seen graduations where you have chairs for their space department. Will there be anything like that? And I was a little surprised by the answer, which is no. One of these speeches will have an actual audience beyond uh, you know, the camera crews that are there to, uh, to capture it and the handful of journalists. Uh, so that's tough. I mean, I think that's gonna be, I guess one way to think of it is the response to the state of the union address. There's always a disadvantage to the party that's out of power because the president comes into the, to the house chamber. You know, there's all that energy, all those people, the applause, all of that. And then, you know, the out-of-party response is usually in a studio. Now, what various people have done this have tried to do over recent years is to do it in front of some other audience. But they never can quite replicate the audience of the house chamber. But in this case, there's no audiences whatsoever. How is that going to play out?",
