  {
    "episode_id": 77270,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/7/77270.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 14,
    "turn_text": "I think it's going to be weirder than live sports. I've gotten used to baseball already with the fake sound from fans or the cardboard cutouts and the stands are just the normal sounds of baseball. The sounds of politics involve crowd noise and particularly the conventions. They were about those loud demonstrations. They were about the big, you know, speeches that have, kind of, waves of response throughout the arena. All of it crashes together with the beauty, the majesty, the moments we remember all involve a proud noise. And so, yeah, you're giving a speech, looking at a camera. We've all gotten used to Zoom to some extent, but it is a unique challenge in this era. It is, someone who's made the analogy to me about, you remember when Saturday Night Live did those live shows from home? And",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_02",
