    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/7/77270.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 18,
    "turn_text": "Well, we're coming to you with a special edition on Monday and it is quite a lineup because it runs the ideological spectrum from left to right. You've got Bernie Sanders all the way to John Kasich. And Bernie speech I think is probably the most consequential for where the party is at this moment because For all the ways that Biden closed down the primary, he never did it with the progressives on board. And the selection of Kamala Harris has been very popular, according to our polling. Progressives, though, have never really trusted a thought of Kamala Harris as one of them. So the signals from Bernie, I think, are critical here. We're expecting a couple hundred delegates to vote against the Democratic Party platform. Just as a matter, of course, not a huge deal. It doesn't change anything, but their main objection is it doesn't include Medicare for all. Bernie Sanders doesn't love everything in this platform. doesn't love everything about Joe Biden or Kamala Harris. But he is standing up there to kick off the prime time hour, the part that's going to be on network television for the Democrats. And on the other end of that is Michelle Obama. She's the closer on night one, and to me, her speech is all about voter motivation. We remember four years ago where she said, you know, they go low, we go high. Does she bring that back in some way? Or is this just about you got to get out? Because we got to get out and vote. Because you know that Democrats are engaged in this election. They see the stakes. It's not a hypothetical anymore in terms of squaring up against President Trump. How you corral that? And the messaging from the middle is interesting. We're going to talk to Senator Doug Jones from Alabama, an early Biden supporter in just a moment. But from everyone from Jones, a red state Democrat, to John Kasich, an actual real Republican who ran for president four years ago and was a top contender for the presidency himself, who has now said it's actually imperative to elect Joe Biden.",
