  {
    "episode_id": 77270,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/7/77270.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 51,
    "turn_text": "Well, look, this is a message of unity, but now I want to make sure everybody understands that, you know, we're, this convention is not like any convention in the past. I am not going to be standing in front of 10,000 people screaming and hollering and people holding a placard sand go dog or whatever. That's not happening. There's no bright lights. There's nothing like this. There's no cheering crowds. And the time limit has been cut down considerably, I mean, we're like way down. So this is more like a video, video remarks than a rousing convention speech. The theme is clearly, you know, we the people. It is a unifying speech. It will harken to, and not candidly, I don't even call it a speech. I just call it remarks because it is relatively short. I think that even, you know, the President, Michelle Obama, Barack Obama, Those folks have longer periods of time, but we're talking about just a two-hour block for a whole bunch of speakers. So you can imagine this is relatively short. But what I'm talking about is I'm harkening back to the days of despair in Alabama, the Montgomery Bus Boycott, the church bombing in 1963, the Edmund Pettus Bridge, but those things that people found common ground and unified around. and bringing people and how far we've come and Joe Biden being that unifying force. And I'll talk a little bit. I'll mention the fact that I've known him for 40 years and always believe he was going to be a great president. But that's the things you're going to hear from I think everybody tonight is to try to rally around and having someone at the top of the ticket that can really bring this country together and not divide us. That is the most important thing I think that can come out of this convention that we are a diverse country with diverse voices and diverse people. And we have got together around together. We've got to come together as a whole. You know, in Alabama, my theme is one Alabama. It's not an us versus them. It's not a good versus evil. Like my opponent keeps saying, that's where I think you're going to see this entire convention going. And it's somewhat of, you know, it's part of my short remarks tonight.",
