  {
    "episode_id": 77270,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/7/77270.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 53,
    "turn_text": "You know, well, first of all, there is a uniqueness about Biden because I think Joe represents the broad coalition that is the Democratic Party. And I'm not sure Bernie really represents that. I think he represents an important piece and important voices in the Democratic Party. Make no mistake about that. But I think Joe represents and has that ability to bring folks from all sides together. So in that context, I think it's unique. I don't know what role I would have. I would like to think that if Bernie or Elizabeth had been the nominee, that they too would be reaching out to folks, the moderate folks, the more conservative folks in the party, to help them because they would need these votes. They would need people that support me. to try to support them. So it's hard to say, but I did believe all along. I kept telling folks, you guys in the media, I got asked all the time in the Capitol, oh my God, where's Joe? What's going on? It seems like the voices on the far left have the momentum. I kept saying be patient. I believe Joe Biden is where the Democratic Party as a whole is. And I think that that was born out to be true. And I think it's going to be born out that way for the country in November.",
