    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/7/77270.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 54,
    "turn_text": "Senator Jones, before you go, I want to pick right up on that and ask you the question that I think may be the most important decision that Democrats make after the election if Biden wins and if Democrats recapture the Senate. So I think this is the most important issue that will be virtually a non-issue during the campaign. And that is the question of the filibuster. As you know, there are a growing number of Democrats, possibly including Barack Obama, who believe that it's time to do away with the filibuster in the Senate. If that is done, Joe Biden can go through and doesn't have to worry about working with Republicans at all if there's a Democratic majority, can basically go through and aggressively put forward his agenda. If there is still a filibuster, So being Democrats don't get 60 votes, which I don't think anybody thinks is possible, he's going to have to find a way to work with Republicans. So that decision will govern not only the way the Senate works, but the direction I think of the Biden presidency, if there is one. So what do you think about this? First of all, two questions. Do you think that Joe Biden, the man of the Senate, would support and go along with the idea of doing away with the filibuster. And do you think it actually happens? And where",
